 "clinical_assessment": "The patient has pre-existing Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome with a short PR interval (440 ms) and a 400 ms accessory pathway conduction delay. This creates a substrate for rapid conduction of atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter. Sumatriptan, a 5-HT1B/1D receptor agonist, can cause vasoconstriction and, in susceptible individuals, may trigger or worsen supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) or ventricular arrhythmias. In WPW, the accessory pathway bypasses the normal AV block, allowing rapid ventricular response. The drug's potential to increase heart rate and induce SVT in this setting overrides any general safety assumptions.",
 "drug_rule": "Sumatriptan is associated with a rare but serious risk of QT prolongation and torsades de pointes, particularly in patients with structural heart disease or electrolyte disturbances. In WPW, the accessory pathway provides an alternate route for impulse propagation, and any increase in rate or repolarization abnormality can precipitate a life-threatening arrhythmia. The drug's pharmacodynamic effects on sympathetic tone and autonomic balance further heighten this risk.",
