 "drug_rule": "Alpha-blockers reduce peripheral vascular resistance and can cause hypotension. Latanoprost, while primarily an agonist of the uveoscleral outflow pathway, also has mild alpha-1 receptor activity. Co-administration may produce additive hypotensive effects, increasing the risk of symptomatic hypotension, syncope, or falls. Additionally, alpha-blockers can blunt the pressor response to prostaglandin agonists, theoretically reducing the efficacy of latanoprost in treating elevated intraocular pressure.",
 "patient_fit": "Although latanoprost is generally safe, the patient's ongoing terazosin therapy overrides the usual assumption of safety. The combination elevates the risk of hypotension, which is particularly hazardous in a 70-year-old male with BPH who may already be on other medications and has a history of falls. The ophthalmologist's awareness of the alpha-blocker is appropriate, but the interaction itself is the decisive hazard.",
